<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalingga Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">594 CE – 732 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Javanese kingdom on the north coast of Central Java, famed in Chinese records for the strict justice of its Queen Shima.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chinese histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> describe a kingdom called Ho-ling on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> that sent envoys from the 7th century. Most historians place it on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>. Its beginnings are not recorded, and the register's dates are approximate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-shima">Queen Shima</a>, chosen in 674, whose rule was so just that a bag of gold left in the road went untouched for three years until her own son touched it with his foot, for which she ordered his punishment. Ho-ling was also a Buddhist centre: the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huining-monk">Huining</a> worked there with the Javanese scholar Jnanabhadra in 664 to 665 to translate scriptures.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kalingga's later history is obscure. Its people and court traditions are thought to have passed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjaya-of-mataram">Sanjaya</a> in 732 in the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>. The legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-shima">Shima</a> remains a popular story of good government in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalingga-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kalingga Kingdom" width="320" height="376"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalingga_Kingdom_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>594 CE – 732 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shima</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism and Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Holing, Ho-ling, Kalingga</dd></dl></aside></div>
